<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsu-iluna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsu-iluna was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. Shortly after his accession he faced a major rebellion of southern cities led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-ii">Rim-Sin II</a>, which he crushed at the cost of destroying the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>. The Babylonian Empire profile notes that his reign also saw the breakaway of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sealand-dynasty">Sealand Dynasty</a> in the south, halving the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna.jpg" alt="Samsu-iluna" width="312" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Budge, E. A. Wallis (Ernest Alfred Wallis), Sir, 1857-1934; King, L. W. (Leonard William), 1869-1919 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Record_of_the_sale_of_land_in_the_reign_of_Samsu-Iluna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
